          title: "Makarios — congratulations to all the wrong people",
          body: "**Μακάριος** is not a churchy word. It is the ordinary Greek felicitation — *fortunate, enviable, to be congratulated* — the word for people whose life has visibly come off well. That is precisely the shock: Jesus stands up and congratulates the **poor in spirit** (πτωχός is the beggar's word — destitute, not merely modest), the mourning, the meek, the persecuted — every category the ancient world (and ours) files under *unenviable*. Note two structural facts. The list is **description, not prescription**: these are not entrance requirements to attempt, but an announcement of whom the arriving kingdom finds first — read them as commands and you will try to mourn on purpose. And the tenses frame everything: the first and eighth beatitudes say the kingdom **is** theirs, present tense, while everything in between is future — the congratulations are issued *now*, in the middle of the grief, on the strength of what is coming. It is either the most beautiful nonsense ever spoken on a hillside, or the world's actual accounting, read out early.",
