      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a5.22.1',
          kind: "translation",
          refs: ['g5.22.23', 'g5.22.37'],
          lemma: "Ῥακά … γέεννα",
          title: "Raka, and Gehenna — two words we refuse to translate",
          body: "**Ῥακά** is Aramaic street-abuse (*reqa*, “empty-head”) that Matthew himself left untranslated for his Greek readers, so we leave it for you — the graded absurdity of the saying (playground insult → supreme court; “fool” → the fire) works best when you can hear the register. And **γέεννα** is not “hell” but a place you can stand in today: **Ge-Hinnom**, the Valley of Hinnom, curling around Jerusalem's south-west — on this site's 3D map, it is the trench just below Herod's palace. There the kings Ahaz and Manasseh burned children to Molech (2 Kgs 23:10; Jer 7:31), and Jeremiah renamed it the Valley of Slaughter; by Jesus' day it was the city's image of final ruin — later tradition says its refuse fires, though the archaeology for that is thin. Translating it “hell” imports a millennium of later doctrine into a first-century place-name. The saying's actual claim is stark enough without the imports: contempt is murder's seed, and calling a brother worthless plants you in the valley where children were once called worthless.",
        },
      ],
