      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a5.28.1',
          kind: "translation",
          refs: ['g5.28.12'],
          lemma: "πρὸς τὸ ἐπιθυμῆσαι",
          title: "“To covet her” — the tenth commandment, not the glance",
          body: "The verb is **ἐπιθυμέω** — the exact word the Greek Old Testament uses in the tenth commandment, *you shall not **covet*** — and the construction (πρὸς τό + infinitive) marks purpose: looking *in order to* covet. This is not the involuntary noticing that centuries of anxious readers have flogged themselves over; it is the cultivated, acquisitive gaze — attention deployed to possess. Jesus is doing here exactly what he did with murder: tracing a command (the seventh) back to the command that guards its root (the tenth), locating adultery's origin where Exodus already located it — in the wanting, before the taking. The saying convicts nearly everyone honestly examined; it does not convict having eyes. The difference matters pastorally, and the grammar, for once, is the pastoral care.",
        },
      ],
