      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a5.45.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g5.45.11', 'g5.45.20'],
          lemma: "τὸν ἥλιον … βρέχει",
          title: "The theology of weather",
          body: "The argument for loving enemies is not that it works, nor that they deserve it, but a claim about **what God is already doing**: sunrise and rainfall, delivered daily and indiscriminately to the people currently persecuting you. This cuts against a very natural theology — the one Job's friends held, where weather and welfare track desert — and Jesus states the alternative as observable fact: providence is *promiscuous*. To love an enemy is therefore not heroic excess; it is joining the family business (“that you may become **sons** of your Father” — sons in the idiom's sense of *those who show the parent's trait*). For the reader who struggles with petitions and proofs, this may be the Sermon's most accessible claim: whatever else is uncertain, the sun does rise on the unjust — the generosity is checkable. The question the verse poses is whether that is an accident of physics, or a character reference.",
        },
      ],
